<article class="env-proposition" id="02FP">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02FP">Proposition <span data-tag="02FP">6.2.5.7</span>.</a> Let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an inner fibration of simplicial sets. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $U$ is a cocartesian fibration and, for every edge $e: C \rightarrow C'$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the covariant transport functor $e_{!}: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_{C} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_{C'}$ admits a right adjoint. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $U$ is a cartesian fibration and, for every edge $e: C \rightarrow C'$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the contravariant transport functor $e^{\ast }: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_{C'} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_{C}$ admits a left adjoint. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> Moreover, if these conditions are satisfied, then the functor $e_{!}$ is left adjoint to $e^{\ast }$ for each edge $e: C \rightarrow C'$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We will show that $(1) \Rightarrow (2)$ (the reverse implication follows by a similar argument). Assume that $U$ is a cocartesian fibration. Applying Lemma <a data-tag="05Q8" href="/tag/05Q8">6.2.5.6</a>, we deduce that every locally $U$-cartesian edge of $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ is $U$-cartesian. It follows that $U$ is a cartesian fibration if and only if it is a locally cartesian fibration. The implication $(1) \Rightarrow (2)$ now follows from Corollary <a data-tag="06MB" href="/tag/06MB">6.2.5.5</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
